(but one \(or both\) of the parties then exchanging relies on the good) Tj T*
(faith of some one else to fulfil in the future a pledge expressly or) Tj T*
(impliedly made in the prior exchange. Commonly some external evidence) Tj T*
(of the pledge is created and passed at the time, but this is not) Tj T*
(essential to the validity of the pledge itself. For example, A buys 50) Tj T*
(bushels of wheat of B, and B takes in pay for it A's note of hand at) Tj T*
(six months for $75. The note is not the pledge, but it is a legal and) Tj T*
(convenient proof of it. As a case in Value, the wheat is sold for the) Tj T*
(pledge and the pledge is the equivalent of the wheat. Each party) Tj T*
(rendered the other then and there satisfactory equivalents. All our) Tj T*
(definitions apply here perfectly.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Still a further and future exchange was contemplated by both parties) Tj T*
(at the time of making this exchange, and as a silent part of it. A) Tj T*
(takes what is now his own wheat, and B takes as an equivalent for what) Tj T*
(was his wheat a right to demand of A in six months an equivalent for) Tj T*
(the present equivalent \(the pledge\) for the sake of which B rendered) Tj T*
(the wheat. The note of hand is the evidence of this pledge, and it) Tj T*
(belongs absolutely to B. It is his property. He may keep it till) Tj T*
(maturity and then sell it to A for its face, or he may sell it at once) Tj T*
(to a bank for its face less the discount for six months. Discount is) Tj T*
(the difference between the face and the present price of a note of) Tj T*
(hand. The first peculiarity, then, of Credit is, that it always) Tj T*
(involves the element of future time. But it involves this secondarily,) Tj T*
(and not primarily. In other words, a present equivalent is always) Tj T*
(rendered by both parties in every commercial transaction; but the) Tj T*
(present equivalent in the case of a credit transaction is the right to) Tj T*
(demand something of somebody sometime in the future. This distinction) Tj T*
(is very important, as we shall see clearly when we come to treat of) Tj T*
(Banking, though it is generally ill-understood at present. Valuables,) Tj T*
(when they exchange at all, exchange once for all. But there is one) Tj T*
(kind of valuables, namely, claims, which, when subject to exchange,) Tj T*
(imply and require another and a future exchange, not necessarily) Tj T*
(between the parties to the first exchange, but between _some_ two) Tj T*
(parties; and not, speaking strictly, to _consummate_ the first) Tj T*
(exchange, because that took and gave its own satisfactory equivalents;) Tj T*
(but, as involving both time and trust, the credit sale must in the) Tj T*
(nature of things be followed by another sale of one of the three) Tj T*
(kinds.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We see, accordingly, that in Credit our science of Economics takes) Tj T*
(partial possession of future time for certain purposes of its own.) Tj T*
(Exchange sets its throne and reigns pre-eminently in present time; but) Tj T*
(its sceptre extends also over past time, so far as all capital is) Tj T*
(concerned, and so far as all material commodities \(the result of past) Tj T*
(work\) are exposed for sale in the present; and its right hand of rule) Tj T*
(goes forth also to grasp the future, under limitations indeed both as) Tj T*
(to the stretch of time covered and as to the character of the persons) Tj T*
(concerned, but still there is there a fair domain and a broad domain,) Tj T*
(and a realm on the whole winning a wider and wider circuit. It is one) Tj T*
(of the proud boasts of Political Economy as a science, as it is too) Tj T*
(one of the exalted traits of human nature, that the lordly impulse to) Tj T*
