(means of all his money and all his credit; and forms of indebtedness) Tj T*
(are multiplied on every hand. By and by it begins to be perceived in) Tj T*
(certain quarters that the matter has been overdone; speculative) Tj T*
(purchases cease; banks become particular whose paper they discount;) Tj T*
(men find it difficult to sell their debts due in order to provide for) Tj T*
(their debts owed; they fall back on the sale of their commodities, but) Tj T*
(when holders are anxious to sell, prices always fall; a panic now sets) Tj T*
(in, more irrational, if possible, than the previous overconfidence;) Tj T*
(their inflated credits and commodities collapse in the hands of their) Tj T*
(holders; sales at great sacrifices are inadequate to meet the mass of) Tj T*
(maturing debts contracted when confidence was high; men fail, and must) Tj T*
(fail; the banks cannot help them, or think they cannot; and so) Tj T*
(wide-spread commercial disaster comes in.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Such commercial crises swept over the United States in 1837, 1857, and) Tj T*
(1873; and will doubtless recur in the time to come. They always arise) Tj T*
(from disordered credits, and though not necessarily connected with) Tj T*
(credit-money, are much more likely to come in connection with that.) Tj T*
(The more strong and conservative the Banks maintain their ordinary) Tj T*
(condition, the more powerfully can they operate to prevent or abate a) Tj T*
(panic. They ought always to be on the shore and never in the stream.) Tj T*
(From the very nature of banks and of the motives that create and) Tj T*
(operate them, they are apt to sell for a profit in ordinary times) Tj T*
