(CHAPTER VII.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(TAXATION.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Political Economy is the Science of Buying and Selling. It must) Tj T*
(include of course in its discussions the Motives, the Methods, the) Tj T*
(Obstacles, the Rewards, relating to Sales, which are themselves first) Tj T*
(to be defined as furnishing the sole Field of the Science. We have now) Tj T*
(gone through with painstaking all of these topics in order, but we) Tj T*
(have not yet fairly struck Taxation, which is indeed in all its forms) Tj T*
(an obstacle to Sales, and in some of them the annihilation of Sales,) Tj T*
(but which in its nature is something much more than an obstacle,) Tj T*
(namely, a Condition of something higher than itself. In the very) Tj T*
(strictest sense of the terms, Taxation is not a part of the Science of) Tj T*
(Political Economy, because it is not an essential part of any one of) Tj T*
(those natural processes by which men buy and sell and get gain. It is) Tj T*
(rather a Condition through Government of the successful ongoing of all) Tj T*
(those processes. There cannot be, therefore, a _science_ of Taxes, as) Tj T*
(there is unquestionably a science of Sales. The facts of Taxes are) Tj T*
(artificial and governmental, the facts of Sales are natural and) Tj T*
(original.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(All forms of Production, as we have now seen, go forward in accordance) Tj T*
(with positive natural forces and motives, which God has appointed, and) Tj T*
(which men have a natural impulse to ascertain and generalize and) Tj T*
(profit by; for it is Nature bids men work and save, buy and sell,) Tj T*
(invent and transport, navigate and grow rich; but Nature has given no) Tj T*
(whisper anywhere, at least that we can hear, about any Taxes. That is) Tj T*
(the work of Society. That seems to be something negative, not) Tj T*
(positive, so far as Buying and Selling is concerned. Taxation is) Tj T*
(indeed something necessary to the social order, as men are; it) Tj T*
(furnishes means of defence against greater evils than itself is; but) Tj T*
(in itself considered, it is an economic evil, because it takes away) Tj T*
(from exchangers a part of the gains of their exchanges; strictly) Tj T*
(speaking, therefore, it cannot be made a part of Economic Science.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(But, on the other hand, as we shall see at length in the exposition) Tj T*
(that follows, all the relations of Taxation from the beginning to the) Tj T*
(end are so ultimately connected with Exchanges, are so founded on and) Tj T*
(limited by Exchanges, its true principles are so exclusively) Tj T*
(economical, and its abuses are so instantly and constantly harmful to) Tj T*
(all the ongoings of natural and profitable Trade, that Taxation must) Tj T*
(always be treated as if it were a part of Economics. The latter is a) Tj T*
(science, the former is an art; but the art is almost exclusively) Tj T*
(dependent upon the principles of this one science; and a comprehensive) Tj T*
(treatise on the science, accordingly, must exhibit all its main) Tj T*
(bearings upon those practical rules of Taxation, which are so vital to) Tj T*
(the happiness and prosperity of any People. All scientific Economists,) Tj T*
(therefore, have considered the subject of Taxes to lie within their) Tj T*
