(Prof. Perry's most elementary text-book, Introduction to Political) Tj T*
(Economy, presents the subjects of Value, Production, Commerce, Money,) Tj T*
(Credit, and Taxation, in a way plain and easily grasped by young) Tj T*
(minds, but at the same time scientifically exact. In his preface the) Tj T*
(author says: "I have endeavored so to lay the foundations of Political) Tj T*
(Economy in their whole circuit, that they will never need to be) Tj T*
(disturbed afterwards by persons resorting to it for their early) Tj T*
(instruction, however long and however far these persons may pursue) Tj T*
(their studies in this science.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     "This work is not meant in any way to take the place of its) Tj T*
(     author's larger treatise, but rather to occupy a field which,) Tj T*
(     in the nature of the case, that work cannot occupy. It is not) Tj T*
(     an abridgment of that work but a separate treatise, intended) Tj T*
(     primarily for the use of students and readers whose time for) Tj T*
(     study is small, but who wish to learn the broad principles of) Tj T*
(     the science thoroughly and well, especially with reference to) Tj T*
(     the scientific principles which are involved in the practical) Tj T*
(     discussions of our time.... We need scarcely add, with) Tj T*
(     respect to a writer so well known as he, that his thinking is) Tj T*
(     sound as well as acute, or that his doctrines are those which) Tj T*
(     the greatest masters of political science have approved.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(                                 --_The N. Y. Evening Post._) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Prof. Perry's Advanced Work._) Tj T*
() Tj T*
